<section class="chapter" id="author-note"><header><h2>Author’s note</h2></header>
<p>This book contains short articles that speak about my thoughts, reflections, and observations about life, human behavior, choices, purpose, and the realities we face as humans.</p>
<p>It is not written to please anyone or to force opinions on anyone, but simply to express what I have seen, experienced, and understood over time.</p>
<p>Readers are free to agree, disagree, or reflect, but the intention remains the same: to make us think deeper about life and the choices we make every day.</p>
<p class="closing">Isaac Frank</p>
<nav class="pn"><span></span><a href="#c1">Next: There Is Always a Choice to Make</a></nav></section>
<section class="chapter" id="c1" data-t="There Is Always a Choice to Make"><header><p class="num">Chapter 1</p><h2>There Is Always a Choice to Make</h2></header><p>Choice is the ability to choose from different or available options. So far as there are options available, then a choice can definitely be made. One of the things we often tell ourselves or use to deceive ourselves as humans is that we always want the options to be in our favor or positive, and that's not how it is.</p><p>Sometimes, I hear people say things like, "Oh, I'm left with no choice," "That was the only choice I had," and so on. But is this really true, or is it just our human fantasy?</p><p>From my little study and observation, I've come to discover that no matter what we're passing through, we always have options we can pick from. The problem that comes with choosing from these options is the consequences of whatever we choose.</p><p>One of my classmates back in high school posted a quote some years ago which says, "Slaves had choices to make either to die free or to live in chains as slaves." Did you get it? They could either have fought for their freedom even if it would have cost them their lives or stay alive and become slaves. But they chose the latter, which is to stay alive and live in slavery, hoping that one day they'll become free. Some of them actually died as slaves, while a few later gained their freedom.</p><p>Life itself is a product of the choices we make today, whether consciously or unconsciously. And sometimes, because we make unconscious decisions or find ourselves between two unfavorable options, we think that we didn’t or don’t have choices to make.</p><p>I’ll try to explain three types of options we make our choices from, and you’ll get to know that at one point in time, you may have experienced it and maybe even had conflicts in your thoughts about which choice is preferable.</p><p>The first type of options we may encounter is when you're faced with two or more positive options to pick from. In this case, it is easy to make a choice because all available options are in one’s favor. The only problem is choosing the best among them.</p><p>For example, if you are told to choose one from a PS5, an iPhone, a camera, and a laptop for free, you'll definitely look for the one that is of utmost importance to you and pick it. In this scenario, since all the available gadgets are useful, there may be a little difficulty in picking the best one, but one will choose with comfort.</p><p>Sometimes in life, we are faced with different options which may all be favorable, and what is needed is just for us to make our best choice. Although we don't usually see things like this happen often, when we do, you may never hear people complaining about having no choice.</p><p>Rather, they'll just say they picked the most preferred one among many options The second type of options we may encounter is when we are faced with a positive and a negative option. For example, if you prepare two different types of meals—fried rice with plantain in one pot and beans with plantain in another—and you ask someone who doesn't like beans to choose, he'll definitely go for the fried rice and leave the beans.</p><p>In this scenario, there won't be any conflict of thought because one meal is what the person likes, and the other is what he doesn't.</p><p>We also encounter situations like this in our everyday lives, and at this point, it is very easy to make a choice because we will naturally ignore what isn't favourable and go for what we like. Another example is when you ask a student if he wants to pass or fail his exam. Definitely, his answer will be that he wants to pass and not fail, because failure itself is not a pleasant thing. The third type of options we may encounter is when there are two or more negative options to pick from. Making a choice in this case is the most difficult, and this is when you'll hear people say they don’t or didn’t have any choice.</p><p>This statement is actually wrong because there were options to pick from, but since none of them were favorable, they felt they had no choice.</p><p>Looking back at the quote about slaves, they were faced with two negative choices: to either live as slaves or to die as free men and women. It is said that a living dog is better than a dead lion, so many believed staying alive even as slaves was better than dying.</p><p>We've also read about kings who chose death rather than surrender and become slaves. They couldn't stand stooping low to slavery, even though it cost them their lives.</p><p>I once read about Nelson Mandela there was a time he was offered release on the condition that he would give up his fight for South Africa’s freedom. As much as he needed his freedom, he turned down the offer and chose to remain in prison until he was eventually released without such conditions.</p><p>Sometimes, we make choices for temporary relief while ignoring long-term consequences, or we endure present pain hoping to enjoy future gain.</p><p>No matter what we face, we should always consider the consequences of our actions whether they will make us or mar us.</p><p>I believe that no matter what we're going through, we have an opportunity to make a decision. I chose to write this at this time instead of doing something else; you chose to read this book instead of maybe sleeping or going through social media.</p><p>Someone once asked me, "What about those who were in a building that collapsed and died—did they choose to die?" My answer was that, as emotional as it is, the truth is that it was their choice to be in that building at that time, even if it wasn't convenient.</p><p>You may ask, "What if they were kidnapped?" Then the question becomes: where were they before they were kidnapped? If they hadn’t been in that place, they wouldn’t have been taken there.</p><p>As strict as it may sound, you'll notice that our earlier choices often play a part in where we currently are.</p><p>Making a choice may be difficult most times as humans, that's why we need God’s help and guidance to make the right ones.</p><p class="closing">Let’s not forget that there is power in the choices we make, and those choices can either influence us positively or deform us.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#author-note">Author’s note</a><a href="#c2">Next: Suicide Is an Option</a></nav></section><section class="chapter" id="c2" data-t="Suicide Is an Option"><header><p class="num">Chapter 2</p><h2>Suicide Is an Option</h2></header><p>Life itself is an examination or should I say, life is an exam question with many options. However it suits you, just know that life itself has many options. You'll rarely or never see a question with the same options.</p><p>Just like our simple mathematics back in primary or elementary school, if you're asked to solve for x in the equation 2x + 3 = 5 (assuming it's an objective question), you'll be given options maybe in the form A, B, C.</p><p>Where option A is 3, option B is 4, and option C is 1.</p><p>If you're good at solving mathematical problems, you'll definitely know that the correct option is option C. But what happens to the other options? Or do you think everyone will choose option C?</p><p>No, everyone can't it is totally or almost impossible.</p><p>Do you know that some people might confuse the plus sign as minus? Do you know that some might not even know that the 3 will cross over to meet 5 and change to minus 3? Some don’t even have the thought that the sign will change.</p><p>As simple as that question looks to you, it is a complex question to someone else. That is exactly how LIFE is.</p><p>While you'll choose option C, someone else might go for option A or B. Why? It is either they are confused or they don't know the correct answer.</p><p>That is how life seems to be with different options that may even look alike, but most times only one is correct.</p><p>Why do people commit suicide? This is a question we'll never stop asking ourselves as long as we exist, but most times we find it hard to answer.</p><p>I once met a boy from Europe online, and this guy wanted to commit suicide because he was or is gay. But you know what? He was afraid to die.</p><p>I don’t know if he actually went through with it.</p><p>He wanted to commit suicide because he thought it was abnormal (which we all think) to be gay and that it wasn't accepted. He saw himself as an outcast and maybe someone who was useless.</p><p>I remember when he chatted with me, I could feel he needed help on how to live in his environment, but he didn't want to stop being gay. At some point, I sensed he was depressed. Before I continue, let me ask: what do you do when you feel that someone is depressed? Do you just look away or give a word of encouragement without any follow-up?</p><p>Some of us tend to judge those who commit suicide, but how helpful or observant were we when depression started?</p><p>So many things are popping into my head right now, but I'll try not to go far.</p><p>Don't you know that most people see suicide as the best option because of what their environment says about them? Don't you know most people think of suicide because they believe it is the perfect answer to their pain? Don’t you know that to some, suicide looks like freedom from this wicked world?</p><p>Ask those who are suicide survivors why they wanted to end their lives, and you'll see that their answers are often shocking.</p><p>I know of a lady who wanted to commit suicide because her parents wanted to choose her husband against her will. This lady is a female activist and also into motivational talks—but you may ask, why did she want to commit suicide? Couldn't she motivate herself? Couldn't she stay strong and fight?</p><p>Maybe I'll say you don't know what is called DEPRESSION—the robber of destinies. It is no respecter of anybody, not even pastors.</p><p>Have you ever been in a situation where even your own turn their back on you and it seems that God is silent? What do you do? What comes to your mind at that time?</p><p>Of course, I don't support suicide, but have you asked why people commit suicide?</p><p>Some years ago, a motivational writer committed suicide. I went through her write-ups and saw it wasn't just poetry, it was depression speaking. She poured out her thoughts.</p><p>But what happened? Little or nobody cared to ask her why she wrote what she wrote. Maybe they thought it was her usual style of writing.</p><p>Oh no even a motivator sometimes needs motivation.</p><p>Just like the mathematics question mentioned earlier, such is life. We have to choose from the options depending on our understanding of the question. Not all options are correct, but there is definitely an answer.</p><p>As we try to fight depression, we also need to be an inspiration to many.</p><p class="closing">SUICIDE IS AN OPTION BUT DEFINITELY NOT THE RIGHT ANSWER. BE A LIFE TO SOMEONE TODAY.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#c1">Previous</a><a href="#c3">Next: Why People Commit Suicide</a></nav></section><section class="chapter" id="c3" data-t="Why People Commit Suicide"><header><p class="num">Chapter 3</p><h2>Why People Commit Suicide</h2><p class="sub">From my own point of view</p></header><p>As I have always said and will continue to say, people sometimes see suicide as an option in moments of deep pain. That is, suicide is often the result of a personal decision made during a very difficult period in one’s life.</p><p>People have been asking and will continue to ask: why did he commit suicide? Why did she commit suicide? Why did they commit suicide?</p><p>“I thought he was a motivational speaker.”</p><p>“He should have known how to handle the situation.”</p><p>“Was he not a pastor?”</p><p>“He should have understood that life has trying times.”</p><p>But why did he still put the rope on his neck? Why did he pull the trigger? What led him to pick up that knife?</p><p>These are questions people ask every day.</p><p>Suicide has never and will never be the right choice but why do people still see it as an option?</p><p>I once read about a pastor who committed suicide because he thought God was no longer with him. He felt empty, and it seemed as if God was silent.</p><p>But guess what? This same pastor used to encourage people, telling them that even when God seems silent, they should never give up.</p><p>Yet he still took his own life.</p><p>What a world we live in.</p><p>Even motivational speakers and writers are not left out. The strongest person can still fall victim. People commit suicide because of the terrible situations they find themselves in. Maybe they failed in some areas, maybe they feel poor, maybe they struggle with low self-esteem, or many other reasons.</p><p>Do not allow anyone to deceive you into thinking that money or fame can save you from suicidal thoughts. That is a lie.</p><p>There was a time when one of the richest men in the world committed suicide. Why? He simply said he was tired of life.</p><p>How can a rich man be tired of life? You may ask. But he lacked something maybe peace of mind.</p><p>His wealth could not give him the peace he needed, so he became overwhelmed.</p><p>The reason I can give is this: people often focus only on the dark side of their situation without looking at the bright side. And this has sent many people to early graves.</p><p>If all you see are dark clouds in the night without noticing the moon, you may never find light to guide you.</p><p>So I ask you when you look at the night, do you only see darkness, or do you also see the moon and stars?</p><p>I will conclude here.</p><p>There is a difference between believing that the future is bright and knowing that the future is bright.</p><p>I will not explain it fully here but think about it.</p><p>For example, from a Christian perspective: can you say everyone who believes in Jesus truly knows Him?</p><p>If you can answer that, you will understand the difference between believing and knowing. Do you truly know the future is bright, or are you just believing it?</p><p>Sometimes, when we don’t understand things deeply, we end up believing blindly. It is good to believe but it is better to understand.</p><p>You might end up feeling like a failure or even fall into suicidal thoughts if you only believe without truly knowing your purpose.</p><p>Save a soul today and live a life that gives breath to others.</p><p class="closing">Suicide is never the answer.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#c2">Previous</a><a href="#c4">Next: Watch the Weight</a></nav></section><section class="chapter" id="c4" data-t="Watch the Weight"><header><p class="num">Chapter 4</p><h2>Watch the Weight</h2></header><p>Cecilia, a final-year student of Economics, was about to defend her project to an external examiner whom she had not met in person. It was the day of her project defence, and she had planned to get to the venue 30 minutes earlier.</p><p>On her way to the venue, she met a man who asked her for directions because he wanted to get to MONGO PARK. Mongo Park is a popular place in the school—so popular that even the birds that fly around are familiar with it. Cecilia was surprised that this unknown man, or rather a strange man, claimed not to know the place.</p><p>She answered rudely and said, "Hey guy, as you can see I'm going somewhere, and I don't have any time to waste at all, so get away from here. Are you trying to toast me all in the name of asking for directions? You didn't even ask for another place it’s the popular MONGO PARK you're asking of."</p><p>"Hmm, I am …."</p><p>"Spare me that crap," Cecilia interrupted, not allowing the young man to complete his statement. "Excuse me," she hissed, and walked away, leaving the young man embarrassed.</p><p>About five minutes to the commencement of the project defence, Cecilia was shocked to see the same young man step into the venue. She got the shocker of her life when she discovered that he was her external examiner. At that moment, she knew she was in serious trouble and feared he wouldn’t tolerate any mistakes from her due to her earlier reaction.</p><p>The young man simply smiled at her and told her to begin her presentation.</p><p>Her presentation wasn’t good in any way—because of the shock and maybe stage fright, she made several silly mistakes. The unknown man, who was later introduced as Prof. Michael Ajani, who seemed to be in his early forties, later narrated the incident that occurred between him and Cecilia.</p><p>He said if she had responded calmly, he might have assumed she was shy or afraid of the crowd. But with the way Cecilia confronted him earlier, he was 100% convinced that her mistakes were inexcusable, and she would have to return the next year when she was fully prepared to defend her project.</p><p>Did you get it?</p><p>Cecilia’s rude response to Prof. Michael cost her an extra year. Most of us are like Cecilia we do or say things without considering the outcome. Oh! we think it's normal, or maybe we act out of ignorance, desperation, or even for fun. But the question is:</p><p>DID YOU WATCH THE WEIGHT OF WHAT YOU DO? Or you just do them? That relationship you're about to enter have you weighed it? Can you carry it, or will it weigh you down? The business that looks attractive and promising have you watched and weighed it? It works for others doesn't mean it will work for you. What about friendships, your character, the things you do daily, or what you're planning to do in the future have you watched the weight? Just like our human bodies, where we sometimes skip meals and exercise to stay fit, maintain balance, and look good our lives also require us to skip some things and add some things. If you don't watch the weight, you're exposing yourself to mistakes that could have been avoided. Life is a risk, but that doesn't mean we should make foolish decisions.</p><p>Always remember yes, humans are bound to make mistakes, but if those mistakes can be avoided, it is better to avoid them than to make them.</p><p class="closing">As much as you'll say that mistakes bring experience, also remember that some mistakes can lead to destruction or deep regret.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#c3">Previous</a><a href="#c5">Next: The Liability Mindset</a></nav></section><section class="chapter" id="c5" data-t="The Liability Mindset"><header><p class="num">Chapter 5</p><h2>The Liability Mindset</h2></header><p>It was Bob Marley that said, "Emancipate yourself from mental slavery, none but ourselves can free our minds." This lyric, which is from the album Redemption Song, has truly taught us that we can either choose to be enslaved by our minds or choose to control it.</p><p>Some time ago, while I was busy with laundry, I heard a whisper as usual (as I do hear most times): THE LIABILITY MINDSET. It took me some seconds, and later minutes, to actually understand a part (not in full) of what this means, and that's what I’ll try to talk about from my own perspective. Also, this is not exhaustive.</p><p>I've always said and will continue to say that it is the mindset that determines the human way of life that is, the way you think is the way you live, either positively or negatively, or maybe in a balanced way.</p><p>Most of us have this liability kind of mindset, in which we try to make others look inferior while we appear superior. In fact, the liability mindset can also be termed as the selfish approach towards life. This selfish approach has made or should I say has given a lot of people the belief that they are meant to be receivers and not givers.</p><p>A great man once said God gave us two hands one for receiving and the other for giving but it is so unfortunate that most of us just want to remain on the receiving side and not the giving side.</p><p>We’ve cultivated this mindset to the point where we behave as if people owe us something in one way or another. Let’s use an everyday example.</p><p>In a relationship between a guy and a lady, it is believed that once a guy is in a relationship with a lady, he is expected to cater for her wants and needs. Sorry to say, this belief has caused serious damage to some ladies’ way of thinking. Some ladies will even sit idle, doing nothing, expecting that a rich guy will come from nowhere and take care of them.</p><p>The moment you tell some ladies, “I love you and I would love us to be in a relationship,” that same moment you automatically become their bank and ATM. Little wonder most relationships these days are based on lust and not love (guys are also guilty of this).</p><p>My dear, nobody owes you anything except maybe your parents, who have the responsibility of taking care of you to a certain age.</p><p>If you get angry because people are not giving you money when you ask, without even considering whether they have it or not, my brother, my sister you are exercising that liability mindset.</p><p>If you are the “I need money and not people” type, you are slowly piling up your liabilities. A part of the liability mindset is placing more value on wealth than on people. I’ve heard people say, “If you’re not talking money, you’re not making sense.” But the truth is, such people will only go far if they carry people along.</p><p>Do you know that money can become a liability? Maybe not now, but in the future.</p><p>One of the greatest assets you can acquire is having the right people around you. Money is good in fact, money answers many things but money can never give you everything.</p><p>Have you noticed that some people are in your life because of what you have? And the moment what you have is gone, that is the end of the relationship.</p><p>Oh, life indeed is a crocodile, as I would say.</p><p>Do you know that a lot of us have lost money because of the people we neglected or are still neglecting?</p><p>I remember hearing the words: Treat everyone you meet today as the connection you may need tomorrow.</p><p>God may not come down from heaven to answer your prayers; rather, He may use people like you and me.</p><p>Have you ever asked yourself: what will happen if I choose money over a person who could have brought me more value tomorrow?</p><p>Money may attract people to you, but what if their intentions are not genuine? How will you discern?</p><p>Also, most of us believe that life is about give and take that if I give you, I must receive from you. But I would rather say life is about give or take. It’s either you are giving or you are receiving.</p><p>Most people help others today because they expect help in return tomorrow. But what if tomorrow comes and the person doesn’t help you back? Won’t you feel disappointed or even call them a bad person?</p><p>It is said that one good turn deserves another but does that mean one good turn owes another? When we begin to have this expectation mindset, we gradually become a liability.</p><p>Don’t be shocked if you help someone today and they don’t help you tomorrow. That doesn’t necessarily make them a bad person that’s just how life can be sometimes.</p><p>When you do good and expect it back, you may end up being disappointed and gradually develop the liability mindset.</p><p>Have it in your mind that little or nobody owes you anything even after you have done nice for them.</p><p>If we were to live based on expectations, then why are we told not to repay evil with evil? If you value money more than people, you are living a liability kind of life. And if you are someone who constantly expects from others or believes people should always give to you, you are slowly becoming a liability.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#c4">Previous</a><a href="#c6">Next: The Way You See Life Is the Way You Will Live Life</a></nav></section><section class="chapter" id="c6" data-t="The Way You See Life Is the Way You Will Live Life"><header><p class="num">Chapter 6</p><h2>The Way You See Life Is the Way You Will Live Life</h2></header><p>Life is complex that as humans, we see it in different ways, and therefore we have different perspectives about it. If you ask 10 people what they feel or think about certain things, you'll see that all of them may have perspectives that are different from one another. These perspectives enable individuals to give meaning to whatever situations they may find themselves in. Our definitions of happenings in life give us a template or understanding of how we handle them.</p><p>A lot of people have become victims or victors based on the perspective they have towards life. A positive perspective towards life enables one to live well, no matter what life throws at that person.</p><p>I once met a lady online in 2020 from one of the African countries. She felt depressed and was already contemplating suicide. During my conversation with her, I got to know that her perspective about what she thought was the problem wasn't in the right direction. I encouraged her, told her to see beyond what she was experiencing, and ensured that she focused more on the positive sides rather than engaging in the negative parts of life. After our conversation, she was really happy and indeed got the message.</p><p>I read a story where a lecturer brought a piece of white paper with a black dot on it to class. He asked his students what they could see in his hand, and almost all of them had a similar response—that they could see a black dot on the white piece of paper. They all ignored that the paper was white and only dwelt on the black spot on it. The lecturer then asked them why they left the whiteness of the paper and only focused on the black spot. What he was trying to teach them is that we focus so much on the dark side of life that we ignore the brighter side. The reason why most people end up as failures is not just because they failed, but because they decided to accept and see themselves as failures. Life sometimes may not give us what we want, but life will definitely give us what we choose from its options. Our choices will be dependent on our perspective of life.</p><p>Life may not offer us sweetness all the time, but we must ensure that we enjoy the sweet things that life gives. Life may be funny, but we must ensure that it doesn't make fun of us. No matter what we pass through or experience, we should always know that change is the only thing that is constant.</p><p class="closing">The lenses through which you view life will determine the picture you capture from it.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#c5">Previous</a><a href="#c7">Next: Treat People the Way You Want Them to Treat You</a></nav></section><section class="chapter" id="c7" data-t="Treat People the Way You Want Them to Treat You"><header><p class="num">Chapter 7</p><h2>Treat People the Way You Want Them to Treat You</h2></header><p>The golden rule states that “do to others what you want them to do to you.” As simple as this rule may sound, I think it is one of the hardest things we can comply with as humans. The world would have been a better place if only we really cared about our neighbors. Most times, we are only interested in what we want to gain from people, and we don’t even bother about what we can give to them. We are quick to judge people by their actions, but we often forget that we also commit similar wrongs, just in different forms.</p><p>Jesus, in one of His teachings, admonished us to love our neighbors as we love ourselves. This was not just a teaching but also a command, which implies that we should always relate with people around us the way we want them to relate with us. We find it hard to love people as ourselves most times because of our greed. If it is not in our favor, then we wish it should not be in the favor of anyone else. This mindset has handicapped humanity, because for us to make the world a better place, we must look out for one another.</p><p>We must always try to put ourselves in people’s shoes before we begin to judge them. Always ask yourself, “What would I want to be done to me in this situation?” How well do I show compassion to people when they need it?</p><p>I’ve noticed that humans are only interested in what they will gain; anything aside that is of no use to them. Do you know that there are people who will sell you out if a price is placed on you, no matter the relationship you share with them? The world is a cruel place where only a few people are willing to become selfless. Treating people the way you would want them to treat you is actually a difficult task, but it is necessary if we want the world to be a better place. To treat people the way we want them to treat us is actually looking out for one another, even when no one is watching. We need to help people without always thinking about what we may gain, but rather focus on how we can make a positive impact in their lives. Always ask yourself this question: “Would I be happy if this thing is done to me?” If your answer is no, then maybe you should rethink the action you are about to take.</p><p>Treating people the way you want them to treat you does not mean you should support them in doing things that are bad. Rather, it is about treating people right, treating them well, and doing good without expecting anything in return. As it is often said, you reap what you sow, and not where you sow. This means that it is not necessarily the person you helped that will help you in return, but maybe someone else you do not even expect at all.</p><p class="closing">Always remember that no matter what you do: the good you do stays with you, and the evil you do comes back to you. In the end, life always reflects what we give into it.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#c6">Previous</a><a href="#c8">Next: The Abundance of Nothing</a></nav></section><section class="chapter" id="c8" data-t="The Abundance of Nothing"><header><p class="num">Chapter 8</p><h2>The Abundance of Nothing</h2></header><p>It was King Solomon who declared in his writings, “Vanity upon vanity, all is vanity.” After all he had enjoyed, he came to realize that there was actually nothing as fascinating as he had once thought. Sometimes, we want to acquire as much as we can, not minding who we hurt along the way. We often forget that whatever we acquire here on earth will become another person’s inheritance when we leave. We are often concerned about what we have materially and forget that material things, though good, are bound to perish.</p><p>I once read a story of a rich man whose wife got sick, and he spent a lot of money to ensure that she received the best medical attention. But in the end, she died. Looking at how much he had spent, he said, “money, shame on you.” This is the same money that many people are killing themselves for, yet it became of no use to the man in that moment.</p><p>Sometimes, what we pursue is totally vanity because we forget that all these things are bound to fade away. We sometimes focus only on how much we have achieved and forget how fulfilled we truly are. We forget that achievement is not fulfillment, and ambition is not purpose.</p><p>In my book DIE EMPTY, I gave a brief explanation of the difference between ambition and purpose. Your ambition is what or who you want to be, while your purpose is who God wants you to be. Your ambition may be driven by your desires, but your purpose is about God’s plan. Achievement goes in line with ambition because it is centered on you what you gain or become which may add little or no value to others. Fulfillment, on the other hand, is about making an impact on people around you and beyond.</p><p>There are many people who, if mentioned today, may not even be remembered. Yet there are others who, even after hundreds of years, are still remembered as if they were alive. They made sure they created a positive impact while they were here. This does not mean that these people were poor, but they saw life beyond the scope of money—they lived lives of transformation. I also read about a scientist who made a discovery and chose not to make money from it, which eventually led him to die poor. While I may not support the idea of dying poor, the man understood that money should not be the ultimate goal, but rather the impact he could make. As much as we want to acquire things, we should also remember that the reason for our existence is to make impact, not to oppress others. The cars, houses, companies, positions, gold, men, and women we acquire today may not have the same value tomorrow. The greatest investment we can make today is in making an impact in our generation by raising people who will continue that impact.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#c7">Previous</a><a href="#c9">Next: Equity, Not Equality</a></nav></section><section class="chapter" id="c9" data-t="Equity, Not Equality"><header><p class="num">Chapter 9</p><h2>Equity, Not Equality</h2></header><p>I am of the belief that life itself is more about equity than equality. A lot of people do not understand that equity can sometimes be better than equality because equity takes into consideration our capabilities, while equality focuses more on giving everyone the same thing, regardless of their ability.</p><p>This does not mean that equality is not important. Equality gives people a sense of fairness and equal opportunity, which is good. But in reality, people are not the same in strength, capacity, or understanding, and that is where equity becomes necessary.</p><p>Even our Creator had a reason for not creating all fingers equal—not because one is more useful than the other, but because each of them has a different purpose. In the same way, human beings are different, and those differences should be considered when things are being shared or responsibilities are being given.</p><p>Jesus, in His story about the talents, gave an illustration of how three different people were given different amounts of talents to trade with. Two of them used theirs effectively, while the last person went ahead to bury his. From this story, you will see that the master gave each servant talents based on their abilities and not because he was partial. He understood that giving everyone the same thing would not produce the same result, so he acted based on their capacity.</p><p>I remember while growing up, my mother would dish our food into different plates and ask us to pick, starting from my eldest sister down to me, since I am the last born. I noticed that the food was not shared equally, but each person took based on how much they could consume. Does that mean my mother was being partial? No, she wasn’t. She simply understood our different needs and gave accordingly to avoid waste.</p><p>Another example is giving a boy of 3 years old and a boy of 13 years the same bucket size to fetch water. At first, it may look fair, but in reality, it is not. The younger one may struggle, not because he is unwilling, but because he does not yet have the strength. Equality, in this sense, gives the same responsibility, but equity considers ability and makes adjustments where necessary.</p><p>That is why when people talk about equality alone, without considering ability, it can sometimes lead to problems. Equality may open doors for everyone, but equity helps people walk through those doors successfully. Equality gives opportunity, but equity supports performance.</p><p>Life itself is about priority and not partiality. This means that what needs to be done should be done based on necessity and not just for the sake of fairness. Equity creates room for productivity, especially when people are placed where they can function best, without unnecessary pressure or comparison.</p><p>A world that focuses only on equality without considering ability may begin to struggle, because people may demand what they are not yet prepared for. But when equity is applied alongside equality, there is balance, growth, and better results for everyone.</p><nav class="pn"><a href="#c8">Previous</a><a href="#about">About the author</a></nav></section>
